-- title:
--   Universal factorisation through φ forces generation by its image
-- statement:
--   Let $A_0$, $A$, $B_0$, $B$ be commutative rings in a single universe, with $A$ an $A_0$-algebra, $B_0$ an $A_0$-algebra and $B$ an $A$-algebra, and let $\varphi : B_0 \to B$ be a ring homomorphism which is compatible with the structure maps in the sense that $\varphi \circ (\text{algebraMap } A_0\, B_0) = (\text{algebraMap } A\, B) \circ (\text{algebraMap } A_0\, A)$ as ring homomorphisms $A_0 \to B$. Assume the universal property: for every commutative ring $T$ equipped with an $A$-algebra structure (in the same universe) and every ring homomorphism $g : B_0 \to T$ satisfying the corresponding compatibility $g \circ (\text{algebraMap } A_0\, B_0) = (\text{algebraMap } A\, T) \circ (\text{algebraMap } A_0\, A)$, there is exactly one $A$-algebra homomorphism $h : B \to T$ whose underlying ring homomorphism satisfies $h \circ \varphi = g$. Then two conclusions hold: the $A$-subalgebra of $B$ generated by the set-theoretic range of $\varphi$ is all of $B$, and, if $B_0$ is of finite type as an $A_0$-algebra, then $B$ is of finite type as an $A$-algebra.
--
--   This is the standard abstract consequence of a base-change (or tensor-product) universal property: an algebra characterised by such a factorisation property is generated by the image of the structural map, and finite type descends along it. It is used in the construction of charts on modular curves, where the relevant rings are recognised only through their universal property.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_Algebra_adjoin_range_eq_top_and_finiteType_of_forall_existsUnique_comp_eq.lean

